        "A practical full-body session can be organized around a squat or step, a hinge, a push, a pull and a carry. That might mean a goblet squat, a hip hinge with dumbbells, an incline push-up, a cable or band row, then a farmer carry. A gym is optional; equipment and exercise choices depend on the body, skill and space available.\n\nChoose versions you can perform with control. Begin lighter than your ego would like. Leave a little in reserve, especially while learning. Add repetitions or load gradually when the current work feels steady. If you have pain, a medical condition or a long break from exercise, get individualized guidance before treating a magazine plan as your program.\n\nThink of the five patterns as a map, not a checklist. A squat gets you down and up; a hinge teaches you to pick something up; a push and a pull balance work across the upper body; a carry asks the trunk and grip to keep working while you move. A chair, sturdy counter, resistance band or pair of household weights can make these patterns more accessible. The right variation is the one you can control safely."
